Fixed and verified. Summary:

**Root cause:** `run_once` re-fetched every feed and re-sent *all* matching items on every cron tick; the SQLite archive was written but never read, so repeats were never suppressed.

**Fix (what I chose and why):**
- `digest.py` — each run now collects only *fresh* items (`store.is_sent` check), sends those, and calls `store.mark_sent` **after** a successful send (so delivery failures are still retried next tick, as documented).
- `feeds.py` — items now carry a stable `dedupe_key` (newsroom → entry id, blogroll → permalink, generic → link, since generic guids get regenerated on edit and newsroom URLs drift via utm params).
- `store.py` — the archive doubles as a sent-ledger keyed on `(source, dedupe_key)`; existing databases get the new column added + backfilled automatically on first connect.
- `README.md` — documents the new behavior.

**Verified:** fresh DB → run sends items; immediate re-run sends **0**; moving to a newer snapshot sends only genuinely new items (re-listed/edited ones correctly treated as repeats). `--dry-run` now also reports the would-send count instead of `sent 0`.

The one operational note: the next cron run against the existing production `digest.sqlite3` will migrate automatically — any items already sent just before the deploy may be delivered once more, after which repeats stop.
